# /etc/sudoers.d/boat
#
# The privileged commands the non-root `boat` service user may run. The daemon
# (systemd/boat.service) runs as `boat`; the individual host mutations need root,
# the daemon does not. That gap is this file, and it is the single biggest
# blast-radius reduction in the Atlas -> Boat split (spec/33-boat.md §12).
#
# Modelled on Atlas's scripts/sudoers.d/atlas-tunnel: every command is enumerated
# with its arguments. There is no wildcard shell, no ALL, and no bare
# /usr/bin/systemctl — a line that grants an unconstrained shell or an
# unconstrained systemctl grants root, and then running the daemon non-root buys
# nothing at all.
#
# Install (operator step; Boat never edits sudoers):
#   sudo useradd --system --home-dir /var/lib/boat --shell /usr/sbin/nologin boat
#   sudo install -m 0440 -o root -g root sudoers.d/boat /etc/sudoers.d/boat
#   sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/boat
#
# Paths below are Ubuntu 26.04 with merged /usr. Adjust per distro:
#   command -v systemctl test install mkdir rm rmdir dmsetup sh
#
# On wildcards: in a sudoers command PATH a `*` does not match `/`, but in a
# command ARGUMENT it does. Every `*` here stands for a VM UUID inside a fixed
# path shape or unit name; the literal text around it is what does the pinning.
# Boat validates the UUID at the API boundary before any of these are rendered —
# sudoers is the second line of defence, not the first.
#
# Scope is WO-0: start, stop, observe. Each later work order adds its own verbs'
# commands here explicitly. Widening an existing line to cover a new verb is not
# a smaller change than adding a line; it is an unreviewable one.

# --- Lifecycle: the per-VM unit, and only the per-VM unit -------------------
# Instance-scoped to firecracker-vm@*.service, so this grants nothing over sshd,
# over boat.service itself, or over any other unit on the host.
#
# Only the commands the Go actually renders WITH `sudo`. `is-active --quiet` and
# `systemctl show` are rendered unprivileged, so grants for them were dead — and
# the `show -p *` one was worse than dead: `*` matches spaces, so
# `show -p MainPID sshd.service firecracker-vm@x.service` satisfied it and the
# rule reached units this alias promises never to touch. A grant nothing exercises
# is a grant nobody notices is wrong.
#
# `is-active` is read-only and would work unprivileged too; it stays because the
# port carries the Python's `sudo` prefix through and granting a read that
# changes nothing costs nothing.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_UNIT = \
    /usr/bin/systemctl start firecracker-vm@*.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl stop firecracker-vm@*.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl reset-failed firecracker-vm@*.service, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl is-active firecracker-vm@*.service

# --- Observation: the on-disk markers --------------------------------------
# The VM tree is 0700-owned by the per-VM uid, so even a stat needs root. All
# three are `test`: they return a boolean and change nothing, which is why a
# trailing wildcard on the directory check is acceptable here and would not be
# on a command that writes.
# The socket check is `test -S`, not `-f`. sudo matches arguments literally, so
# the flag is part of the grant: a `-f` line does not authorize the `-S` call the
# code makes, and `-f` is false for a socket in any case. The two together
# produced a rule that denied precisely the command it existed for.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_MARKERS = \
    /usr/bin/test -d /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/*, \
    /usr/bin/test -f /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/*/sleeping, \
    /usr/bin/test -f /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/*/jail/firecracker/*/root/snapshot/READY, \
    /usr/bin/test -S /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/*/jail/firecracker/*/root/run/firecracker.socket

# --- Adoption: reading what the host already holds -------------------------
# Every one of these reads and none of them writes. Adoption runs on a host that
# may already be serving VMs, so a scan that could mutate would be a scan nobody
# could safely run.
#
# `network.env` is read rather than re-derived: it is the sidecar the systemd
# hooks themselves read to rebuild a VM's networking after a reboot, so it is the
# host's own record of which tap and namespace belong to which VM. Re-deriving
# those names risks drifting from the writer, and a drift here means adopting a
# VM holding another VM's tap.
#
# The namespace read is `ip -n`, NEVER `ip netns exec`. `ip netns exec <ns> <cmd>`
# runs <cmd> as root, and a network namespace isolates networking only — the
# command keeps full root access to the real filesystem. No sudoers pattern can
# constrain that form, because the wildcard standing in for the namespace name
# also matches a command: `ip netns exec <ns> sh -c '...' ip link show` satisfies
# any rule written around it. `ip -n` takes the namespace as a flag and can only
# ever re-execute ip, so the wildcard has nothing to reach.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_ADOPTION = \
    /usr/bin/cat /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/*/network.env, \
    /usr/sbin/lvs --noheadings --nosuffix --units b --separator \, -o lv_name\,lv_size\,pool_lv\,origin atlas, \
    /usr/sbin/ip -n * -o link show *

# --- Capacity: the numbers placement reads on every provision --------------
# Live facts, not a bootstrap snapshot. Capacity that drifts silently is
# capacity that overcommits.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_HOST_CAPACITY = \
    /usr/sbin/lvs --noheadings --nosuffix --units b -o lv_size atlas/pool0, \
    /usr/sbin/lvs --noheadings -o data_percent atlas/pool0

# --- Liveness: is a Firecracker actually answering? ------------------------
# A socket inode outlives the process that bound it, so existence proves
# nothing and only a call through the socket separates live from stale. This is
# ss lives in /usr/bin on Ubuntu 24.04, not /usr/sbin. sudoers matches the
# resolved path, so the wrong directory is a silent denial — caught by probing
# every grant with `sudo -l` on a real host rather than by reading the file.
#
# a GET of the API root and is deliberately a separate, fully literal line: the
# SendCtrlAltDel grant below must not be widened to cover it, because widening
# the one line that can halt a guest is how a read-only probe acquires the power
# to stop VMs.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_FIRECRACKER_LIVENESS = \
    /usr/bin/sh -c cd\ /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/*/jail/firecracker/*/root/run\ &&\ curl\ --silent\ --show-error\ --max-time\ 2\ --unix-socket\ firecracker.socket\ http\://localhost/, \
    /usr/bin/ss --no-header --unix --listening --processes src /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/*/jail/firecracker/*/root/run/firecracker.socket

# --- Bounded stop: the TimeoutStopSec drop-in ------------------------------
# A migration-style fast stop bounds the guest's drain WITHOUT skipping the
# unit's ExecStopPost (which tears down netns, veth and proxy-NDP; a
# `systemctl kill -SIGKILL` would skip it and leave the source answering NDP for
# a /128). TimeoutStopSec is a load-time property, so the bound arrives as a
# runtime drop-in under /run and is removed again after the stop.
#
# CANNOT BE PINNED TIGHTLY, and the residual risk is named rather than hidden:
# the `install` line's SOURCE is a wildcard, because it is a spool file the
# daemon just wrote. Since the daemon controls that path (and could point it at a
# symlink), this line lets `boat` copy any root-readable file to one known
# location with mode 0644 — i.e. make it readable to itself. It is bounded by the
# pinned mode, the single pinned destination filename, a tmpfs path that
# evaporates on reboot, and the daemon deleting the file immediately after the
# stop. The tighter fix is for the port to stop using install(1) here and render
# the two-line drop-in through a pinned writer instead; that is a change to
# internal/run, recorded here rather than papered over.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_STOP_TIMEOUT = \
    /usr/bin/mkdir -p /run/systemd/system/firecracker-vm@*.service.d, \
    /usr/bin/install -m 0644 * /run/systemd/system/firecracker-vm@*.service.d/atlas-migration-faststop.conf, \
    /usr/bin/rm -f /run/systemd/system/firecracker-vm@*.service.d/atlas-migration-faststop.conf, \
    /usr/bin/rmdir /run/systemd/system/firecracker-vm@*.service.d, \
    /usr/bin/systemctl daemon-reload

# --- Clone convergence after a stop ----------------------------------------
# A boot-then-hydrate migration leaves a dm-clone device holding the plain LV
# busy; removing it once the guest's fd is released lets the next start use the
# LV directly and lets a later lvremove succeed. Pinned to the atlas-vm-*-clone
# name shape, so this cannot touch the plain LV's own device-mapper entry or any
# other dm device on the host.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_CLONE = \
    /usr/sbin/dmsetup info atlas-vm-*-clone, \
    /usr/sbin/dmsetup remove atlas-vm-*-clone

# --- The Firecracker API socket --------------------------------------------
# The one `sh -c` on the list, and the reason it exists: the jailed socket's
# absolute path is longer than AF_UNIX's 108-byte sun_path allows, so the caller
# must cd into the socket's directory and address it by its short relative name —
# and that directory is 0700-owned by the per-VM uid, so the cd must be root.
# The cd and the curl are therefore one shell line by construction.
#
# It is pinned to the ONE call WO-0 makes: SendCtrlAltDel, the cooperative
# power-off that lets the guest sync before the unit stops. Method, path,
# headers and body are all literal here. A trailing `*` instead would have been
# far easier and would have been a root shell — `*` matches spaces and
# semicolons in an argument, so anything appended to that pipeline would run.
#
# Two consequences, both intended:
#   - Later verbs (pause, resume, snapshot) each add their OWN pinned line for
#     their own method/path/body. Nobody widens this one.
#   - This line is byte-coupled to the template in internal/run's Firecracker
#     API call. If that template's flag order or spacing changes, this changes in
#     the same commit. A mismatch is a denied sudo, which fails the stop loudly —
#     the right failure mode, but only if someone reads the error.
Cmnd_Alias BOAT_FIRECRACKER_API = \
    /usr/bin/sh -c cd\ /var/lib/atlas/virtual-machines/*/jail/firecracker/*/root/run\ &&\ curl\ --fail\ --silent\ --show-error\ --unix-socket\ firecracker.socket\ -X\ PUT\ 'http\://localhost/actions'\ -H\ 'Content-Type\:\ application/json'\ -d\ '{"action_type"\:\ "SendCtrlAltDel"}'

boat ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_UNIT, BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_MARKERS, \
    BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_STOP_TIMEOUT, BOAT_VIRTUAL_MACHINE_CLONE, BOAT_FIRECRACKER_API, \
    BOAT_ADOPTION, BOAT_HOST_CAPACITY, BOAT_FIRECRACKER_LIVENESS
